WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.360
Nowadays, everyone knows about The Matrix.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.630
But when it first debuted in 1999, filmgoers
weren't quite sure what they were in for.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:12.370
The movie's blend of such a large existential
framework with slick kung fu action scenes

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:17.349
elevated the Wachowskis and had everyone second-guessing
whether they'd take the blue pill or the red

00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:18.349
pill.

00:00:18.349 --> 00:00:21.779
But with two relatively new filmmakers at
the helm, it's a bit of a surprise such an

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:26.830
ambitious concept ever made it to celluloid,
let alone spawned such a cultural phenomenon.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:31.449
So, let's take a look back at how The Matrix
came to be such a central part of the sci-fi

00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:33.910
film world in the first place.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.660
Odd origins

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:41.030
It's safe to say that The Matrix never would
have come to be if not for one really crappy

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.030
film.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:47.650
1995's Assassins was a lazy action vehicle
for Sylvester Stallone and Antonio Banderas,

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:51.149
directed by Richard Donner in full paycheck-cashing
mode.

00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:55.850
But Assassins wasn't just a bad movie...it
was also the Wachowskis' second completed

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:00.840
script, which was ultimately sold to Warner
Bros. In the end, the script itself is what

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.649
earned the pair a deal with Warner Bros.

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:03.649
Taking the helm

00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:07.710
The Wachowskis developed a relationship with
Assassins producer Joel Silver, to whom they

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:12.230
showed their script for The Matrix early on,
and he was immediately blown away.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:16.390
Silver remembered, "The minute I started reading
the script for The Matrix, I wanted to see

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.290
it … but then the [Wachowskis] said, 'And
we want to direct it.'

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.500
That was going to be tough."

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.810
Instead of taking a gamble on a script that
was obviously going to take a beefy budget

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:31.460
to realize, Silver took a more cautious approach:
He greenlit Bound, a smaller piece about lesbian

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.450
lovers on the run from the mob, which the
Wachowskis would write and direct.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:40.170
The film was then made on a tight $6 million
budget and was a hit with critics.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.990
That was almost enough to convince Warner
Bros. to let the newly-minted directors proceed

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:46.540
with their passion project.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:50.969
But there was just one problem: while everyone
seemed to agree that The Matrix was a great

00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:55.510
script, nobody understood the Wachowskis'
vision in the least.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.270
"There is no spoon?"

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.049
So, the siblings had to improvise ...

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:01.500
Storyboarding

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.800
Producers were so confused by the premise
of The Matrix that the Wachowskis turned to

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:10.450
a pair of illustrators named Steve Skroce
and Geof Darrow to present the premise in

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:12.620
a format others might understand.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.300
Skroce handled the storyboards, illustrating
scenes in a dramatic, noir-like style with

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.470
a heightened sense of motion and impact.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.550
Meanwhile, Darrow handled concept art.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:26.970
Now, for the first time, executives were able
to visualize how this crazy script would translate

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:27.970
to the screen.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:31.770
It was enough for Warner Bros. to greenlight
a $60 million budget for The Matrix.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:36.530
And the pre-production artwork was so impressive
that it was collected in a hardcover volume

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:39.470
the year after the film's release.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:40.750
Casting calls

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:44.870
After executives hopped on board with the
Wachowskis' vision, they still wanted to maintain

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.260
some creative control over the project.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:52.010
The cast, in particular, was to be decided
by Warner Bros., even though the Wachowskis

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.200
had some opinions of their own on the matter.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.900
Keanu Reeves was not the Wachowskis' first
choice for the role of Neo.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.620
Instead, they had their eyes on Johnny Depp.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:05.500
Meanwhile, the studio wanted someone like
Brad Pitt, Val Kilmer, or Will Smith, all

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:06.510
of whom turned down the role.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.450
"This is your last chance.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.420
After this there is no turning back."

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:16.400
Neo's casting was eventually whittled down
to a choice between Depp and Reeves, the latter

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.340
of whom Warner Bros. preferred.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:22.830
Eventually, the studio won out with the casting
of Reeves, and at least one star who turned

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.530
it down has no regrets.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:30.290
Will Smith has said that he's glad he turned
down the role, as Reeves ended up being "brilliant."

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:31.900
Fight preparation

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.810
The Wachowskis envisioned the fight scenes
in The Matrix being heavy on fast-paced, close-quarters

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.660
martial arts combat with elements of wire
work.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.530
So, they decided to appeal directly to the
world's foremost authority in martial arts

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:49.500
choreography, Yuen Woo-ping, director of such
classics as Drunken Master and the man who

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.080
made Jackie Chan a star.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.700
Yuen wasn't interested — until he read the
script.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:58.030
Unlike Warner Bros. executives, it took Yuen
almost no time to understand what they were

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:01.860
doing, and he agreed to bring his crew of
trainers to the production, where his work

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.800
began months before cameras ever started rolling.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.730
All of the movie's stars were expected to
perform their own stunts, but most weren't

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.530
exactly known for being on-screen fighters.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:15.681
So they had to spend months training with
Yuen's kung fu crew and wire workers and and

00:04:15.681 --> 00:04:17.549
suffered a few injuries along the way.

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:22.200
And while it was a brutal preparation process,
the ends definitely justified the means for

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.200
these Matrix stars.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.230
Satisfying the suits

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:30.540
It's an often-quoted piece of trivia that
Warner Bros. initially budgeted the film at

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:35.210
$10 million — only to have which the Wachowskis
use it to create a smash opening sequence,

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.840
and convince the studio to fund the full $60
million they needed.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.200
But that's not exactly how it happened.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.770
According to special effects supervisor John
Gaeta, the Australia-based production had

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:49.720
been scheduled for a 90-day shoot, and it
became obvious at a certain point that it

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.200
was falling significantly behind.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.010
Executives were growing frustrated, so they
hatched a plan to clean up the opening sequence

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:59.640
and its visual effects, and add in some temporary
sound effects.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.730
Once that was done, they sent it to the studio
to let them know just how production was coming

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:04.730
along.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:09.600
After that, Warner Bros. immediately backed
off and gave them an additional month to complete

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:10.600
the film.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.690
It was just enough time to make all that movie
magic happen, including the invention of a

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:17.310
new photography technique to capture those
slow-mo bullet shots.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:22.820
Gaeta was inspired by the Japanese film Akira
and music video director Michel Gondry to

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:26.430
improvise his camera techniques to create
those memorable scenes.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:31.830
He used over 100 still cameras and two motion
cameras to create the effect of a shot moving

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:36.730
into extreme slow motion, while the camera
appears to pan around or track the events

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:38.020
at a normal speed.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:42.500
Each sequence that employed the technique
was first digitally simulated, then meticulously

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:46.940
staged with the camera array programmed to
shoot in a specific sequence with precise

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:47.940
timing.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.270
This technique became known as "bullet time"
camera work and is a registered trademark

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:53.270
of Warner Bros.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:58.170
So, not only did they get a full-on franchise
out of the deal, but they also made movie

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:00.170
history with The Matrix.

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:01.530
"Whoa."

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:02.530
Thanks for watching!

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:04.960
